Personal introduction

Flavia: I'm currently working as a type designer. I freelance independently. I have my own foundry, and I collaborate often with other designers, especially female designers. But I started actually as an editorial designer. I graduated in visual communication, and then I moved from Rio to São Paulo to work in this big publisher.

I went from junior designer to art director within ten years. So I had a whole life and career before type. And I think that helped me enormously because, well, first, when you work more as a graphic designer and art director especially, you are collaborating all the time. You are briefing illustrators, photographers, working really close to the journalists and editors of all the pieces you're putting the layouts together.

After ten years of doing that, we had another economic crisis in Brazil. Things here are a bit crazy. And the whole publishing industry started to decline. I guess it was sort of like the paper crisis we know. So a lot of magazines closed, including the publisher I worked for. At that time, I thought I could use this as an opportunity to change careers and start over. I was also quite tired because I mainly worked with fashion magazines, and the pace is insane. You are constantly needing to come up with ideas. So I was looking for something that I could slow down a little bit.

So I moved to New York not knowing exactly what to do, but I always liked type. I did a lot of calligraphy courses still in Brazil, and I’ve messed with a lot of other people's typefaces to build my layouts because sometimes we didn't have the budget to license the fonts that we liked, so we kind of had to hack things together on even InDesign or Illustrator or whatever, just for like a title or something. 

When I was in New York many of my friends were saying, "You should do *Type@Cooper*. It's perfect for you." And I was like, "I don't even have a portfolio to apply. I never drew anything." But I decided it could be a good idea, so I worked on a portfolio for a year. I applied, I got in and then when I was doing the program, I got in love with type design, and then I was like, "Okay, this is what I'm gonna do. I don't know how, but this is it." After the program, I tried my best to get into the industry, like working on my own projects, getting freelance, and now it's been almost 10 years of my second life, as I call it. Because I did Cooper in 2017, so next year, it's gonna be 10 years since I graduated.

