Flavia’s first collaboration in type 

Phuong: I was very shocked to hear that Casual was just finished recently because I’ve heard that it was your first collaboration in type and also your first release. So to see how much time it takes to finish a typeface is definitely crazy to think about. Can you tell us a bit about this first collaboration and how this came about?

Flavia: Yeah, sure. The ZC Casual story is funny because it's a collaboration with my ex-partner. So I was married to a lettering artist and a sign painter. We're no longer together, but we were together for thirteen years. He's also Brazilian, so we moved together to the US. And when I was doing Type@Cooper, he was interested in figuring out also how to do typefaces because as a sign painter, it would be so helpful for him to have, even if it's not crazy well-built fonts, but something that he could put up mockups faster.

I joked that he was doing the program in parallel. So I'll come home, I'll show him the things that I learned. While I was doing my graduation project, Lygia, he was working on ZC Casual by himself. And then after I finished the program, I thought that it could be a really good project for us to work together because there are still some of the technical parts that he was lacking or he was not interested in. So I said, "Well, I can mark your proofs, or I can even jump in and work on things." And that's what we did. 

I also was contacted in parallel. I was contacted by *James Edmondson* from *OHno*, who helped the first designers in *Future Fonts*. At the very beginning, Future Fonts had very display or experimental typefaces, and my graduation project is a text typeface, so I thought I would show them ZC Casual. I asked *Caetano* and he was like, "Oh my God, imagine that would be so cool."

We pitched, they accepted, and then we had to build something to put up. So we put it up in 2018, the first version of it, and then we continued collaborating together. But when we moved to Berlin in the pandemic. Well, there's not so many opportunities for a sign painter in Germany, as we realized, and he was also a little bit burned out at the time, so he decided to do other stuff. Yeah, then the project kind of got in limbo. Whenever I wanted to finish it, I had to have him because I don't have sign painting training. So even if I could do part of building the character set or expanding the family or whatever, I needed someone with the knowledge of how the letters are constructed within the brush to not let me do weird shit. So then in 2021, we separated, but we are still friends. And every now and again, I would ask him, "Hey, do you wanna finish this project?" And he would be like, "Oh, maybe." And never actually opening the files and doing anything. So in 2023, I decided, no, no, I will finish this. I don't know how, I don't care.

Even if I need to bring other people in, this is gonna get done. Because it did really well especially in the first years on Future Fonts and it's still my best seller, so I'm leaving money on the table if I do not finish this font. 

So I asked him one more time, like: "Hey, do you wanna do this?" And he didn't want to. So I asked: "Do you mind if I bring someone else with calligraphy/sign painting background to guide me on this journey?" And he's like: "Yeah, sure, fine." So that's what happened. I brought in someone to help me with production, and that took... We thought it would take a year, but it took two.

And then it finally got out this March. The fonts were done last year, but I had to get someone else to also do mastering because I don't do that part myself. So it took eight years for the whole thing to happen. It was my first collaboration, but I didn't think much of it. Like, wasn't something that I proposed to someone like the other ones. We were just at the same house on computers next to each other, and we thought that could be fun.

Phuong: But as you said, you’ve collaborated in editorial design before type design, right? 

Flavia: Yeah, so for me it was kind of natural because I was used to working with other designers and journalists. So yeah, I could sometimes act more like an art director of the project tell him what to do, and other times I would take it and do it myself.

We learned a lot because in the beginning I also didn't exactly have a plan, like how can we do this? Like technically, like where is the file storage? Like how we're gonna move from one thing to the other thing. Now I have an oiled process, like I use GitHub, I can branch out and I can work together at the same time in one file with another person. But back then, I knew nothing. We were just exchanging things in Dropbox and hoping nothing will break.

What is collaboration?

Mira: Coming from that story and your background, I think collaboration is a big keyword of your practice. So we wanted to ask how would you define collaboration at the minimum or what can be a collaboration at the maximum?

Flavia: Wow, that's a tricky question. Like I said I always collaborated with others. I would say the minimum it's when it's informed the design, but not exactly the person is creating with you.

For example, like if you were designing a story for a magazine, the story will inform what you're doing. So the inputs from the editor, the journalist will be there. You need to listen but there's no one moving the pieces, choosing the typography or the art direction with you.

So it is a small collaboration, but still a collaboration. And then with the Women in Type collaborations that I did with *Inga*, for example, and with *Ayaka*, so with *Jobim* and *Hanae*, we co-designed the typeface. So it's a constant negotiation because we need to figure out the same goal and the same system.

And we are all drawing at the same time. And then sometimes you're not gonna agree, of course, because the creative process is very subjective, and you might wanna do some things in a way, the other person might wanna do some things another way. And you need to figure out which one is gonna be in the typeface in the end, like maybe we love all of these alternatives, but we need to choose one or two. 

I don't know if that's a very straightforward answer, but I would say the smallest part is like when it's just informing, and the biggest is like when you're literally working together on the same thing.

On communication and negotiation

Phuong: And maybe already connected to that, through your collaborations, there must be a lot of different points of view on different things. So how do you communicate or negotiate or make decisions?

Flavia: I think it's very different to each project because it also depends on the other person or people if it's more than one. When I decided to release the three collaborative typefaces for Women in Type as the crazy person I am, I gave myself three months. I had to convince three other people to embark on this crazy journey. It told them: "Hey, do you wanna do this with me? We're gonna start today, and we are gonna release it in May next year." And a lot of people just answered: "Hell no, I can't do this." From the three that I was able to convince, I got three very different projects out of it.

With Hanae, for example, we started the project from scratch, we decided everything together. We asked ourselves what is this gonna be about? Which industry are we aiming for people to use this typeface? So from the briefing we started together. 

With *Octavia*, working with *Tida*, she didn't want to draw at all, so she decided to art direct her own project because Octavia was her final project at Type@Cooper.

And with Inga, she was like: "Oh, I would love to do it, but I don't have any ideas at the moment. And I was like: "Well, I do have an idea I can present to you, and if you like, we can just go on." She liked the idea and Jobim is sort of a revival from Vendome, so we could always refer to the original and decide what we wanna bring in and what we didn't wanna bring in. So it's a different kind of negotiation because there is something that you can look at.

With Hannae, there's no fixed source, we decided where we are taking inspiration from. So Ayaka would say: "Well, I really like the Caslon from the 1700s. I would like to go in this direction." And then I'll be like: "Well, what if we then add this little quirk from Baskerville, or what if we make it a little bit more workhorse like Times New Roman?" But we needed to figure out literally everything, from the amount of contrast, proportions, from zero. So there are more negotiations, I would say. 

And also I wanted to represent people from as many places and perspectives as possible. Because Ayaka's from Japan and the way that she grows up and interacts with typography is much different than me as a Brazilian, which would be much different than someone that is German or American. So everything kind of plays because all of the references come in, like the way that you judge forms and curves and spacing. So I remember with Hanae at the beginning, because Ayaka works as a branding designer, she would space everything super tight, like almost as a lettering, 'cause that's how she used this. And I'm like: No, if you want this to be text you need to open up I don't know, 40 times.

And I didn't have to have this kind of conversation with Inga, for example, because she's also a type designer. So yeah, it's literally very different from project to project, I'd say. 

Phuong: I think it's interesting that there are projects where you work with someone who is practicing mainly as a type designer like with Inga but then there are collaborations with Ayaka or with Caetano, who didn't work in this field before. I think these collaborations would also lead to very interesting results, would you say so?

Flavia: Yeah, exactly. Yeah. Because when you're working with a trained type designer, it's very common that we would reference the same type history and so the process is more linear and more straightforward, and we move faster for sure. But when you collaborate with someone that is a sign painter or branding designer or whatever, they will bring in crazy ideas that you initially would say, "This is not gonna work," and then you make it work. So it’s nice to get out of your bubble. And I think that's why usually when people are starting their projects, they are usually much looser. And then while you learn, you're getting more and more strict in a way.

Women in Type

Phuong: I wanted to ask if you could introduce us to Women in Type, but it seems that you’ve sprinkled it throughout our conversation. So can you add a bit to what you have said about Women in Type if we're still missing something?

Flavia: Yeah, sure. So Women in Type was a reaction to a lot of things. So first, it started because I was constantly being asked about if I knew typefaces done by women. Especially because during the pandemic, I think you know, activism got more attention in general. So with the Black Lives Matters movement and a lot of other things happening at the same time, clients were more eager to feature certain people in certain times of the year. And so every time people come to me and ask: "Hey, I'm trying to find a serif typeface this way for a project, but it needs to be done by women. I couldn't find anything," I thought, "What? What are you talking about? I know of about 20. I can make you a list." So I was always giving lists to people, and I realized, well, maybe I should just put this in a place where I can send a link.

So that's how I started the review. I would just choose the typefaces that I like and write about them. And doing that, I saw a rapid increase in my subscribers in my newsletter. So in months or a year I had a 230% increase in subscriptions. I realized people are really craving this content, but also the women that I was featuring were very grateful and like, "Oh, thank you so much for doing this," because sometimes it's really hard for them to break into the industry.

And it's sort of crazy because if you see the programs, they're usually half and half or even more women than men. But then when you go to the professional setting, it's just men. If you open credits for most typefaces, you're gonna see a list of men, which pisses me off, especially in Brazil. I was thinking, "Why?" You have 10 people listed in the credits, and you cannot find one woman like to work with. It's a club, you know?

Then I kind of like glued this being pissed off about this with being sad about not collaborating with people anymore. And I thought, well, maybe I do the same, like resolve two things with just one project. That’s why I invited the trio. After this, my plan was to build a platform and onboard as many typefaces by women as possible. Of course, I would still curate the typefaces, so it's always high quality. And in parallel with that, I would also want to do a directory, so I can also link to typefaces already released by other foundries.

It's a big project in my mind, but everything takes time and money, so I need to figure out what I'm doing today, and then the next day. For the second phase of Women in Type, I had to then pivot and figure out another way to do things. That's when I decided to launch typefaces of other women that I didn't touch any of it. So I will do a curate and also all of the admin part.

Since it's my foundry, I will help you figure out, like the storytelling, naming, positioning. I also build the structure, and I'll give them, "This is the minimum character set that you need. You can have one style, three or five. It's gonna be an in-progress model." So the whole structure is already decided, so they just need to say yes or no. And I wanted to only have Brazilians for this round, so I could shed light on Latin America. And it was actually harder. I convinced the first three much easier than the second three because here in Latin America, women, they question themselves even harder.

So everybody would say, "I'm not ready. This is not good enough," or whatever. Yeah. And I'd tell them: “Hey, I've been doing this for almost ten years. I'm telling you it's good enough.” But they would not believe it, which made me even more pissed.

