On crediting and ownership

Mira: I wanted to ask about shared ownership. We’ve talked about how much work goes into community projects, and how some people do this alongside a full-time job. When everyone has different amounts of time and energy to contribute, how do you approach shared ownership? And where does individual authorship fit into that collective work? 

Flavia: I think we always need to give credits when credit's due, right? I'm also quite lucky because during my time in the publisher in my first life I worked in this magazine that was the first in Brazil to start crediting designers per article or per story. Before, you would only have credit for whoever wrote the story or if there's a photo, then the photographer or illustrator, but not the person who did the layout or art direct the whole thing. It was not a practice at all. This is early 2000. And I remember it changed a lot of the talk within the designer community here because you could identify the work of certain people even if they moved. So if I now work at Vogue, and tomorrow I'm working at Elle, people will still recognize the work because they saw the credit once.

So that kind of shaped me. I always thought, "This is something that needs to happen." And in type for a long time, this was not how people did it. There was this myth of the Genius designer that would create the typeface, and that's the only credit that you see. Especially with historical typefaces, there were many other people who helped build a type family in different ways, including the physical production, when people worked with metal, wood, or film. But only one person gets the credit. I think it’s only more recently that people have started crediting everyone involved, which is kind of insane. Like, how are we only doing this now? What I do isn’t the same as what the person doing the mastering does, so we need to give that person credit. I can’t do what they do.

I guess there's the ownership, like the creation, right? So if I'm creating the typeface myself, I'm the sole creator, but then I'll have to credit whoever helped me to produce this piece of software. But I can also co-design something, so then I'm sharing that ownership.

But even when I was a student at Cooper it was common that if you were hired by a foundry, then the credit is for the foundry, not the designer. So imagine I would be working under this company, and nobody would know that I designed this typeface. The design is credit for the company. So this is something else that pisses me off, and there's still today cases that we see people not being credited or buried credits. When I was writing the reviews for Women in Type, more often than not, it's really hard to find all the credits, like people burying the websites. So that also makes it harder to find work from women or from other underrepresented people. 

Phuong: I also see this shift nowadays, because I always feel very good when I could credit my friends or the people who I work with, even the guy who printed my brochure because it’s a beautiful thing to have a project that is created by so many hands. 

Flavia: Exactly. Yeah. And everybody will feel part of the project, and they will all represent the project in the same manner, which will only put the project higher up. Because it's much different if you have one person talking than ten.

Phuong: Yeah. I think it's good that the situation is kinda getting reversed now, and credit is now the standard and cool and not a burden anymore. 

Flavia: Hopefully it stays that way. Still, for stuff done in the past, we are still gonna need to have a lot of research done to give these people credit for what they did. But if in the present time we can do the best ourselves, I guess we are for sure in a better place.

What values are non-negotiable in a collaborative environment?

Phuong: My next question to you would be, do you have any values or boundaries that are non-negotiable when you work with someone? And I guess credit is already one, but are there other things you would not want to negotiate when you work with others?

Flavia: Hmm. I think it kind of goes back to something that I mentioned before. In all the work that I do, especially collaboration, I first think that we are people. We are two people exchanging ideas before work, right? So I guess my boundaries are a lot more in the individual level than in the working level, if that makes any sense.

I would not treat someone in a way that I don't like to be treated. So I try to be as open and as straightforward as possible. I like to communicate my ideas in a manner that it's very understandable, but also leave open space for them to do the same and try to extract. Sometimes it's harder for some people than others, so I would not try to overspeak or impose something. I'm also very curious, so I try to see things from as many angles as possible. Then give credit, of course. Yeah, I mean, I think it's literally on an individual level. So I will treat the other person the same way that I wanted to be treated. 

I don't wanna be overstepped. I don't wanna be told what to do. We can figure out together and say, okay, you do this, you do that. Of course sometimes you need someone to put a structure, so it tends to be me because I'm the one inviting these people. So I'll come already with a kind of timeline saying this is what we should do. But if they tell me, "No, this isn't possible," I'm open to hear, "So what's possible to you?" I guess that's why in all of my collaborative typefaces, the processes are completely different. There's nothing that was replicated from one to the other besides the structure and the infrastructure.

Of course, I need to choose one medium, so I'm imposing working on RoboFont because that's the software I work. That's the one imposition. And the way that we can work on GitHub because it's best for sharing files. So even if the person has no experience, I will teach them how it works. But besides that, it's all open to us to have the best time possible. As if I'm at a party or something and I met this person, and we are exchanging and having fun. So I think it's always about having fun and making something good out of it.

An advice for us

Mira: This already leads us to our last question that we ask every of our interview partners, and you can make it deep or not. But yeah, we would like to ask you if you have any advice for us.

Phuong: Could be anything.

Flavia: Anything? Oh, wow. I think I'm... Damn. Anything. I don't know.
But if there’s one thing I’d say, it’s to be yourself, unapologetically. Find your own way and do the things you believe in, even when it’s really hard. Find your community, because it’s easier when you’re not alone. But yeah, especially now with social media, I think people are trying more and more to fit into these little boxes, look a certain way, speak a certain way, and just do whatever’s trendy. And I don’t know, it’s just so fucking boring.
Can we go back to being weird and experiment and... We don't need to earn money from literally everything that we do. Of course, we all need to pay bills, but as creatives, I think we need to find balance and boundaries to express ourselves to the max and be curious.

Try to get out of our bubbles and go to conferences and meet new people. Invent different projects that you can interview people, 'cause this is also probably a really good way to expand. That's something that I have been considering doing for the newsletter. It's like, "Oh, I have all these questions. Let me find a project for them." This is also something I guess I learned if it's not done, you can invent a project to put yourself where you would like to be. I don't know. It is really big question. I hope this makes sense.

Phuong: I can actually relate so much to this, especially being a POC person in design academia. There was a phase where I would try to act the same, dress the same as the German students in the institute so I could fit in…

Flavia: I mean, it's good to have a bubble sometimes, you know. You need to be part of something. It's really hard to just be completely alone in the world. But I think I start appreciating this more and more the further away I move from home. So as you could probably see, I went from this city to six hours away by car, then it was eight hours away by plane. Then it was almost 20 hours by plane. And the further you go, the more you need to explain your whole existence, sort of. So if you are doing this work that was inspired by an artist in your country that people never heard about, you're gonna need to introduce the person, but also the context of that time in that country.

So I start appreciating that more and more because when you're in the bubble, you don't need to do anything. It's already there. But when you move this bubble to a different place, people burst it without knowing, and you realize, "Aha, there's two ways," right?

Like I could go to Germany and only draw some geometric sans and very straightforward work. Or I can bring in Brazilian flavor, but then I need to explain to everybody. But then you realize that they are gonna be a lot more interested in that because that's what they don't know.

I think, like I said, I'm forty-five now, so I have done it all. So I think that's probably what I would say to very young Flavia when I was also kind of guilty of trying to just immerse and go unnoticed. But you kind of disappear that way.

